#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
GIO agent -- a small, dependency-free control service for the game-server box.

Runs the same file on Linux (RHEL/Debian families; stdlib Python 3.9+, bash and the docker
compose v2 plugin are the only dependencies) and on Windows (Docker Desktop; Python 3.10+).
It is the ONLY thing the Relic launcher talks to on a server box. It:
  * reports, per game version, whether the stack EXISTS, is BOOTSTRAPPED, is PROVISIONED and is UP,
  * bootstraps a stack the first time (the vendor bootstrap.sh on Linux; the same eight compose
    steps run from Python on Windows, where the vendor bootstrap.bat ends with `pause`),
  * provisions it: drops in the pre-GAA player save and makes the events it keeps open last
    practically forever where the version's payload ships them, keeps the Spiral Abyss calendar
    covering today, re-points the advertised IP at the WAN address and imports the progress-template
    databases players can start from,
  * starts/stops a version's stack (only one may run at a time -- ports + subnet conflict),
  * proxies GM commands to muipserver, signing them the same way the MUIP tool does,
  * lists the accounts of a version for the admin (GET /server/accounts: the logins from a copy of
    sdk.db, the characters and who is online through one query each -- read-only, never a job),
  * copies account progress between players, or from a progress template onto one (the guids rewritten
    at the guid paths of the version's save schema, payloads/<version>/save_schema.json -- see copy_save; a
    dry run creates nothing),
  * moves accounts with their progress from a stopped server of another version, or from another server of
    the same version kept in a folder, into a running version whose server reads that save (job `migrate`,
    POST /server/migrate; the source is only read, through a throwaway database over a copy of its files),
  * creates player accounts for the admin (job `accountcreate`: any template imported on the
    stack, no signup policy and no quota -- the bearer token is the authorisation),
  * serves the token-less /public/* endpoints the player mode uses (status, self-service account
    creation from a template, GM commands) -- rate-limited and governed by the admin policy (signup
    and player commands are on by default, the admin may turn them off; every progress template is
    offered by default, and one launcher may create up to maxPerPlayer accounts per version, 5 by
    default),
  * replaces the vendor's published MySQL root password and Flask secret key of a stack it prepares
    (the rule the MUIP sign key follows): GIO_MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD /
    GIO_FLASK_SECRET_KEY when set, else a random value per stack -- in .env before a database
    exists, through ONE `ALTER USER` statement once MySQL answers; a value that is not the vendor's
    is never touched. <stack>/.relic-creds keeps the root password the server last accepted (and a
    pending one, written BEFORE its ALTER), so a re-extracted archive -- which brings the vendor's
    .env back -- or a crash in the middle cannot lock the agent out of its own database,
  * leaves each stack's own settings alone while "Keep .env settings" is on (GIO_KEEP_ENV, off by
    default): OUTER_IP in the stack's .env is never rewritten, and the vendor's published MUIP sign
    key, MySQL root password, Flask secret key and stack internal password stay as they are -- a
    change already on record is still finished, a value the database or the running stack really
    uses is still put back, and what the admin asks for (POST /server/secrets) is still done,
  * rotates the stack's secrets on request and toggles in-game password verification,
  * mirrors the official in-version hotfixes (the "hotpatch" outputs of miHoYo's CDN) into a
    folder on the box -- first as one ready-made archive.org bundle (md5-pinned, unpacked in a
    staging folder, only manifest-verified files enter the mirror), then file by file from the
    manifest's upstreams (the official CDN, or a public copy of its outputs where the CDN does not
    hold them) for whatever is left -- serves them token-less under /hotpatch/<path> in the CDN
    layout and makes the dispatch advertise them (server/res/PC_version.txt +
    server/data/version.txt + the four t_region_config URL columns), so every client hotpatches
    itself at login the way the official client did -- plus, per voice language the admin picks,
    the BASE-build voice packs a client that lacks them downloads at that same login (a Japanese /
    Korean / Chinese Windows selects that voice),
  * downloads the ready-made vendor server stack itself from the Internet Archive when the box has
    none yet (job `fetch`: resumable, size + md5 verified, extracted with 7zz/7z/bsdtar or 7-Zip /
    tar.exe, the top folder renamed onto GIO_DIR_xx, the 2.8 nested server/data layout healed),
  * downloads the pathfinding server's data into a stack that lacks it (job `navdata`: ONE archive of
    server/res named by payloads/<version>/navdata.json, size + md5 verified, unpacked beside the
    files it is for and every file proven; only the files the stack does not have go in, a file it
    has is never replaced, and the md5 list the pathfinding server reads names them),
  * verifies every HTTPS download fully (chain + host name) but without the RFC 5280 pedantry Python
    3.13 turned on (VERIFY_X509_STRICT rejects the legacy root a Windows store may end archive.org's
    chain at); GIO_CA_FILE adds roots, and a download whose md5 + size are pinned beforehand may
    repeat over an unverified connection when its certificate cannot be verified
    (GIO_TLS_PINNED_FALLBACK) -- the md5 is what proves the file then,
  * lets the admin edit an allowlist of its own settings from the launcher (GET/POST /agent/config:
    written back into its KEY=VALUE config file -- comments, unknown keys and order kept --, applied
    at once where the code reads them live, the rest flagged for a restart) and restart itself
    (POST /agent/restart: under systemd it exits and Restart=always brings it back with the re-read
    EnvironmentFile; elsewhere it starts a fresh copy of itself with its boot environment),
  * changes a version's stack folder (job `relocate`, POST /server/relocate): the stack is brought
    down under its current project name, then MOVED (a rename, or a verified copy to another drive)
    or the version only RE-POINTED at a folder that holds a stack / none yet, GIO_DIR_xx written
    into the config file and the stack started again when it was running,
  * changes what the game itself hands out, on request (job `tweaks`, GET / POST /server/tweaks --
    the launcher's gameplay settings, Server -> Game rules): the resin cap and its regeneration, the Spiral Abyss enemy
    line-up and enemy level, the enemies a domain spawns and their level, what the domains drop and
    cost, what the quests and the hangout events reward. ONE document per version says what is
    wanted; a data file is always rewritten from its BASE -- the vendor's ORIGINAL (pinned by md5 in
    payloads/<version>/tweaks.json, shipped in payloads/<version>/tweaks-originals.tar.gz, kept in
    <stack>/.relic-tweaks/base before its first rewrite), or the base taken over from a file changed
    by hand --, only while the game server runs and no service that loads those files is still
    booting, and the game server's own loader is the judge: it -- and no other service -- is sent
    SIGHUP once, rebuilds its config from disk without a restart, and its log is read for the
    verdict. A refusal or a missing verdict puts the previous bytes back at once, a stopped stack
    only ever gets originals back, and a Start applies what was saved meanwhile. Settings are
    applied one at a time: a setting whose file cannot carry it waits for a Revert or a Reset of its
    card, which puts the card's files back at once. What an apply that was cut off
    left on disk goes back in front of every docker call of the agent that boots or restarts one of
    those services -- a Start, the watchdogs' jobs, events, txt fixes, a restart for the address,
    the hotpatch or the secrets -- and when the agent itself starts: no game service boots from a
    data file the server has not accepted.

Anything slower than a couple of seconds runs as a JOB: the POST returns {"job": "<id>"} at once
and the client polls GET /jobs/<id>?since=N for the streamed log. One job at a time (only the
download phases of `hotpatch-voice`, `fetch`, `navmesh` and `navdata`, and the extraction of `fetch`
and `navdata`, let the watchdogs' jobs and player signups start beside them).

Security: every admin request needs the bearer token (GIO_AGENT_TOKEN); /health, /public/* and
the static /hotpatch/<path> mirror never do. Two deployment shapes:
  * direct (default for the Relic app): GIO_AGENT_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:18080 -- the app talks straight
    to the agent over the network; the token (baked into the app build) is the only credential.
    Open the port in whichever firewall the box runs (install_agent.sh does this automatically).
  * tunnel (advanced): GIO_AGENT_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:18080 -- reachable only via an SSH local-forward.
The MUIP sign key is read live from muipserver.xml and never leaves the server.

Configuration: environment variables (systemd EnvironmentFile) and/or `--config FILE` (KEY=VALUE
lines; the environment wins). The agent writes that file (--config, else /etc/gio-agent/config) only
through POST /agent/config. `--log FILE` appends stdout/stderr to a size-rotated file (needed
under pythonw, where stdout is None). See docs/AGENT-API.md for the full contract.

Run:      GIO_AGENT_TOKEN=... python3 gio_agent.py [--config FILE] [--log FILE]
Selftest: python3 gio_agent.py --selftest      (no docker / network needed)
Fetch:    python3 gio_agent.py --config FILE --fetch VERSION [VERSION ...]   (asks the RUNNING agent
          to download the server package of each version in turn and streams the job log; exit 0 done /
          1 a job failed / 2 usage / 3 agent busy / 4 agent unreachable)
Relocate: python3 gio_agent.py --config FILE --relocate VERSION DIR [--mode repoint|move]   (asks the RUNNING
          agent to change a version's stack folder -- repoint by default, DIR "" takes the version off; the
          relocation's dry run first, then the job followed; exit 0 done / 1 refused or failed / 2 usage /
          3 agent busy / 4 agent unreachable or the token refused)
Build:    python3 gio_agent.py --build-id      (prints the build identity of these files: one sha256 over
          this script and its payload files -- what GET /status reports as `build`; without --log, and
          with GIO_PAYLOAD_DIR naming the payloads folder when it is not the one a run would take)
Any other option ends the run with the usage line (exit 2): a command meant for another build of the agent
never starts this one beside the running agent.
"""
